BAS Test 0 Simulation Framework Specification

Status: Task 2 only
Purpose: lightweight pre-build causal-pressure model
Scope: locked BAS Test 0 configuration only
Governance state: simulation may expose sensitivities; it may not validate BAS.

1. Simulation Mission Definition

The simulation attempts to estimate:

simulation_targets:
  - buoyancy_state_timing
  - rise_descent_timing
  - restoration_timing
  - q_pump_vs_q_leak_sensitivity
  - stroke_response_over_6_in
  - rod_friction_tolerance
  - drag_effect_on_motion
  - trapped_air_sensitivity
  - partial_fill_metastability_regions
  - pump_timing_phase_sensitivity
  - phase_drift_tendency
  - stall_thresholds

Primary question:

Under simplified assumptions, what variable ranges allow bounded 6-inch vertical cycling, and which variables most quickly destroy it?

The simulation is not allowed to claim:

prohibited_simulation_claims:
  - BAS_is_validated
  - BAS_works
  - stable_actuator_behavior_proven
  - operational_readiness
  - useful_power_generation
  - efficiency
  - scalability
  - dirty_water_viability
  - long_term_reliability
  - physical_repeatability
  - safety

Allowed wording:

“The simplified model predicts sensitivity to X under assumed bounds.”

Not allowed:

“The simulation proves BAS will cycle.”

2. Minimum Variable Set
Variable	Why it matters	Priority
drum_mass	Sets gravitational load opposing buoyancy	Existential
drum_displacement_volume	Determines maximum buoyant force	Existential
submerged_volume	Determines instantaneous buoyancy	Existential
internal_water_volume / fill_state	Determines buoyancy-state transition	Existential
trapped_air_volume	Can shift buoyancy unpredictably	Existential
water_ingress_rate	Controls flooding transition and descent timing	Existential
water_evacuation_rate	Controls restoration and buoyancy recovery	Existential
Q_pump	Determines restoration capacity	Existential
Q_leak	Determines whether restoration can dominate	Existential
Q_pump - Q_leak margin	Core recovery condition	Existential
rod_friction / sleeve_drag	Can consume buoyancy margin or create metastable hover	Existential
fluid_drag	Affects rise/descent timing and damping	Secondary-high
stroke_length	Initial 6-inch boundary; 12-inch later only	Existential
position z(t)	Main motion output	Existential
velocity dz/dt	Indicates transition commitment and damping	Secondary-high
acceleration d²z/dt²	Indicates rebound, impact, instability	Secondary-high
pump_timing	Determines phase relationship with state transition	Existential
pump_lag	Can cause restoration delay or phase drift	Existential
partial_fill_state	Detects hover/metastability	Existential
rebound_coefficient	Determines endpoint state corruption risk	Secondary-high
side_load_proxy	Represents tilt/slosh-induced guide friction	Secondary-high
cycle_count	Required for drift detection	Existential
timing_variance	Distinguishes bounded cycling from transient rhythm	Existential

